1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,830
Imagine that tomorrow, astronomers
detect something strange.

2
00:00:04,741 --> 00:00:06,681
Not just an unusual radio signal.

3
00:00:06,761 --> 00:00:08,261
Not a pulsar.

4
00:00:08,341 --> 00:00:09,831
Not a quasar.

5
00:00:09,921 --> 00:00:11,611
Not some new natural phenomenon.

6
00:00:12,413 --> 00:00:14,323
Something that looks like technology.

7
00:00:15,233 --> 00:00:18,543
Something that is changing in a
way nature doesn't normally do.

8
00:00:19,295 --> 00:00:20,605
Someone made it.

9
00:00:22,968 --> 00:00:25,348
And that would mean that
somewhere out there, another

10
00:00:25,428 --> 00:00:28,958
technological civilisation
exists — or existed.

11
00:00:29,933 --> 00:00:32,403
But then comes the really
interesting question.

12
00:00:33,315 --> 00:00:35,585
How would we actually
communicate with them?

13
00:00:38,345 --> 00:00:41,655
But before we can communicate
with them, we have to find them.

14
00:00:42,323 --> 00:00:47,133
When we think about extraterrestrial
communication, we usually imagine radio.

15
00:00:47,743 --> 00:00:50,303
But there is no reason
to assume that an alien

16
00:00:50,383 --> 00:00:53,973
civilisation would use
exactly the technology we use.

17
00:00:54,752 --> 00:00:56,412
So we should be looking
for technosignatures

18
00:00:56,492 --> 00:00:58,982
across the electromagnetic spectrum.

19
00:00:59,772 --> 00:01:01,582
Radio and microwaves.

20
00:01:02,452 --> 00:01:06,162
Optical and laser signals.
Infrared radiation.

21
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:10,280
And even the chemical fingerprints
of planetary atmospheres.

22
00:01:10,770 --> 00:01:14,160
We don't have to visit a planet
to learn what it is made of.

23
00:01:14,710 --> 00:01:16,060
We can look at its light.

24
00:01:16,906 --> 00:01:20,936
The missing colours in a spectrum
tell us which molecules absorbed them.

25
00:01:21,866 --> 00:01:25,616
And an atmosphere full of industrial
gases could be evidence of technology.

26
00:01:26,689 --> 00:01:29,259
We might never see the
civilisation itself.

27
00:01:29,989 --> 00:01:33,179
We might simply see what its
technology does to its planet.

28
00:01:35,669 --> 00:01:38,019
Now suppose we actually find something.

29
00:01:38,789 --> 00:01:41,979
There is a civilisation five
hundred light-years away.

30
00:01:42,509 --> 00:01:45,619
That sounds relatively
close on a galactic scale.

31
00:01:46,372 --> 00:01:47,852
But it means that the signal we are

32
00:01:47,932 --> 00:01:50,382
receiving left them
five hundred years ago.

33
00:01:51,312 --> 00:01:55,462
If we send a message back, it takes
another five hundred years to arrive.

34
00:01:56,612 --> 00:01:59,441
And then their reply,
another five hundred.

35
00:01:59,891 --> 00:02:02,801
One question. One
answer. A thousand years.

36
00:02:05,656 --> 00:02:10,386
If they are a thousand light-years away,
the round trip takes two thousand years.

37
00:02:11,116 --> 00:02:15,446
Across the Galaxy, we could be talking
about tens of thousands of years.

38
00:02:16,271 --> 00:02:19,201
At that point, this isn't
really a conversation anymore.

39
00:02:20,231 --> 00:02:25,241
It is correspondence between civilisations
separated by centuries or millennia.

40
00:02:26,119 --> 00:02:27,669
We wouldn't be talking to them.

41
00:02:28,159 --> 00:02:32,309
We would be talking to a civilisation
that existed when the signal was sent.

42
00:02:33,179 --> 00:02:34,829
They might already be gone.

43
00:02:35,139 --> 00:02:37,169
Or they might have changed completely.

44
00:02:38,061 --> 00:02:41,201
So realistically, if we detect
a technosignature on the other

45
00:02:41,281 --> 00:02:47,531
side of the Galaxy, our first reaction
probably won't be this. "Let's call them."

46
00:02:48,207 --> 00:02:50,237
It will be this. "Record everything."

47
00:02:53,007 --> 00:02:57,777
Even if the signal is clearly artificial,
we still have to figure out what it means.

48
00:02:58,671 --> 00:03:02,181
We might recognise that there
is information encoded in it.

49
00:03:02,891 --> 00:03:04,661
We might find repeating patterns.

50
00:03:05,711 --> 00:03:09,061
We might even discover something
that looks remarkably like a grammar.

51
00:03:09,885 --> 00:03:12,135
But none of that tells
us what the symbols mean.

52
00:03:12,325 --> 00:03:15,115
And this is where things
get really strange.

53
00:03:17,725 --> 00:03:20,965
Because communicating with
an alien civilisation might

54
00:03:21,045 --> 00:03:23,455
be a little like trying to
communicate with dolphins.

55
00:03:24,563 --> 00:03:26,413
We know dolphins are intelligent.

56
00:03:27,223 --> 00:03:29,273
We know they communicate with each other.

57
00:03:29,963 --> 00:03:32,473
We can record their sounds
and look for patterns.

58
00:03:33,443 --> 00:03:35,493
We can study their behaviour for decades.

59
00:03:36,526 --> 00:03:38,086
And yet, we still don't have anything

60
00:03:38,166 --> 00:03:40,876
resembling a normal
conversation with them.

61
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:43,160
And dolphins are right here.

62
00:03:43,770 --> 00:03:45,050
We share the same planet.

63
00:03:45,130 --> 00:03:46,920
We evolved in the same biosphere.

64
00:03:47,870 --> 00:03:50,180
We can watch what they
do. We can experiment.

65
00:03:51,186 --> 00:03:53,026
And even with all of that, understanding

66
00:03:53,106 --> 00:03:55,916
their communication is
extremely difficult.

67
00:03:57,143 --> 00:04:00,953
Now imagine doing the same thing
with an alien civilisation.

68
00:04:01,763 --> 00:04:03,333
Except we don't share a planet.

69
00:04:04,163 --> 00:04:06,113
We don't share an evolutionary history.

70
00:04:06,971 --> 00:04:08,901
We may not share the same senses.

71
00:04:09,651 --> 00:04:12,201
We may not even share
the same basic concepts.

72
00:04:13,031 --> 00:04:16,261
And they could be hundreds or
thousands of light-years away.

73
00:04:17,055 --> 00:04:19,815
Suddenly, communicating
with aliens starts to

74
00:04:19,895 --> 00:04:21,605
look less like calling
another civilisation...

75
00:04:23,789 --> 00:04:25,309
...and more like trying
to have a conversation

76
00:04:25,389 --> 00:04:28,119
with a dolphin that
lives in another galaxy.

77
00:04:31,229 --> 00:04:34,679
There is another reason human
communication works so well.

78
00:04:35,149 --> 00:04:37,979
We share an enormous
amount of common knowledge.

79
00:04:38,815 --> 00:04:42,405
If I tell you this. "A dog
is sitting on the table."

80
00:04:43,179 --> 00:04:45,399
You immediately understand
a ridiculous amount

81
00:04:45,479 --> 00:04:47,789
of information that I didn't actually say.

82
00:04:48,619 --> 00:04:49,969
You know what a dog is.

83
00:04:50,239 --> 00:04:52,169
You know what a table is.

84
00:04:52,759 --> 00:04:56,129
You understand objects,
gravity, bodies and perspective.

85
00:04:57,105 --> 00:04:59,475
And if I show you a
photograph of that dog,

86
00:04:59,785 --> 00:05:02,315
you also understand what a photograph is.

87
00:05:02,925 --> 00:05:07,595
You understand that the thing inside the
image isn't actually inside your computer.

88
00:05:08,446 --> 00:05:10,066
None of this needs to be explained.

89
00:05:10,146 --> 00:05:12,476
Because we share the same world.

90
00:05:13,257 --> 00:05:14,747
An alien doesn't.

91
00:05:17,571 --> 00:05:20,141
Their language might not
have nouns and verbs.

92
00:05:21,091 --> 00:05:24,701
Maybe those categories simply don't
make sense in their way of thinking.

93
00:05:25,477 --> 00:05:31,027
Maybe their communication isn't a sequence
of symbols at all. Perhaps it is spatial.

94
00:05:31,897 --> 00:05:36,247
Or visual. Or based on changes
in electromagnetic fields.

95
00:05:37,142 --> 00:05:40,762
Maybe they communicate things that
we don't even have concepts for.

96
00:05:40,842 --> 00:05:43,812
And the problem works in both directions.

97
00:05:44,622 --> 00:05:47,152
They might be looking at our
transmissions and thinking.

98
00:05:48,142 --> 00:05:49,492
"What on Earth is this?"

99
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:52,990
And that would be a perfectly
reasonable question.

100
00:05:54,495 --> 00:05:57,185
Suppose we transmit a photograph of a dog.

101
00:05:58,055 --> 00:05:59,765
But what are they actually receiving?

102
00:06:00,515 --> 00:06:02,825
A pattern of electromagnetic radiation.

103
00:06:03,917 --> 00:06:07,007
We could spend years explaining
the concept of a dog...

104
00:06:08,411 --> 00:06:10,711
...only to discover that
the alien equivalent of

105
00:06:10,791 --> 00:06:14,401
"dog" isn't even a meaningful
category in their world.

106
00:06:17,061 --> 00:06:21,391
Biology also determines how fast
organisms process information.

107
00:06:22,561 --> 00:06:26,071
An alien civilisation could have an
entirely different subjective timescale.

108
00:06:27,804 --> 00:06:31,814
Imagine a species whose thoughts happen
a thousand times faster than ours.

109
00:06:32,624 --> 00:06:35,934
Our idea of a "conversation"
might be meaningless to them.

110
00:06:38,234 --> 00:06:41,144
And this may be the most
disappointing possibility.

111
00:06:41,254 --> 00:06:45,324
Even if we discover intelligent
extraterrestrial life...

112
00:06:46,761 --> 00:06:49,171
...we may never have a
conversation with it.

113
00:06:49,801 --> 00:06:52,571
We might only ever receive
messages from its past.

114
00:06:53,494 --> 00:06:56,214
We could spend generations
trying to understand a signal

115
00:06:56,294 --> 00:07:00,464
that was transmitted before
our civilisation even existed.

116
00:07:01,334 --> 00:07:05,404
But that doesn't make the signal
useless. Quite the opposite.

117
00:07:06,454 --> 00:07:10,744
It could be the most valuable scientific
object humanity has ever discovered.

118
00:07:11,567 --> 00:07:13,757
Something made by another intelligence.

119
00:07:16,707 --> 00:07:20,737
This is where artificial intelligence
becomes extremely interesting.

120
00:07:21,747 --> 00:07:25,037
An AI wouldn't necessarily
understand the alien message.

121
00:07:25,467 --> 00:07:28,757
But it could be extraordinarily
good at finding structure.

122
00:07:29,671 --> 00:07:32,131
Give it enough data and it
could search for repetitions.

123
00:07:32,211 --> 00:07:38,081
Patterns. Correlations. Hierarchies.
Possible symbols. Possible grammar.

124
00:07:38,921 --> 00:07:43,531
It could test millions of hypotheses that
would take humans centuries to explore.

125
00:07:44,308 --> 00:07:47,828
And if we had other observations
of the same civilisation.

126
00:07:48,548 --> 00:07:52,258
Its planet, atmosphere, energy
use, orbital structures.

127
00:07:53,188 --> 00:07:56,058
We could begin connecting the
message with the physical world.

128
00:07:56,824 --> 00:07:59,514
Not because AI magically
understands aliens.

129
00:08:00,524 --> 00:08:02,224
But because it can
search an enormous space

130
00:08:02,304 --> 00:08:05,394
of possibilities far
faster than humans can.

131
00:08:07,824 --> 00:08:09,324
And if the signal were real, we probably

132
00:08:09,404 --> 00:08:11,994
wouldn't keep the problem
inside one laboratory.

133
00:08:13,184 --> 00:08:18,454
Imagine releasing it to the world.
Millions of computers. Scientists.

134
00:08:19,184 --> 00:08:22,614
Amateurs. Universities. AI researchers.

135
00:08:23,567 --> 00:08:27,417
Different algorithms competing to
find patterns in the same signal.

136
00:08:28,187 --> 00:08:30,937
It could become something
like SETI@home on steroids.

137
00:08:31,547 --> 00:08:35,057
And there would be one thing we
would absolutely not want to do.

138
00:08:35,785 --> 00:08:37,115
Delete the data.

139
00:08:38,863 --> 00:08:42,293
We would want the original
observations. Every frequency.

140
00:08:42,503 --> 00:08:45,933
Every spectrum. Every
timing detail. Everything.

141
00:08:46,791 --> 00:08:49,531
Because the analysis
techniques of the future might

142
00:08:49,611 --> 00:08:52,241
find something that we
completely missed today.

143
00:08:52,988 --> 00:08:55,468
We might end up with an
astronomical archive containing

144
00:08:55,548 --> 00:08:59,918
more raw data than any scientific
project in human history.

145
00:09:02,688 --> 00:09:05,658
And suppose the aliens aren't
trying to teach us physics.

146
00:09:06,508 --> 00:09:08,338
Suppose they send us entertainment.

147
00:09:09,314 --> 00:09:14,134
A soap opera. Advertisements.
Sports. Music. News.

148
00:09:14,214 --> 00:09:16,164
Something completely mundane to them.

149
00:09:16,714 --> 00:09:18,944
It could be incredibly valuable to us.

150
00:09:19,677 --> 00:09:21,697
A soap opera might reveal relationships.

151
00:09:21,777 --> 00:09:27,807
Social hierarchies. Humour. Jealousy.
What they fear. What they desire.

152
00:09:28,695 --> 00:09:31,185
An advertisement might
tell us what they value.

153
00:09:32,095 --> 00:09:35,665
A sports broadcast might tell us
about competition and cooperation.

154
00:09:36,835 --> 00:09:40,135
And suddenly, something that
was completely ordinary to them

155
00:09:40,215 --> 00:09:43,045
becomes an extraordinary window
into an alien civilisation.

156
00:09:45,425 --> 00:09:46,625
And of course...

157
00:09:48,003 --> 00:09:50,393
...they could do exactly
the same thing to us.

158
00:09:51,158 --> 00:09:54,198
And if our television broadcasts
ever reached them, they might

159
00:09:54,278 --> 00:09:57,238
be trying to figure out why
humans spend so much time watching

160
00:09:57,318 --> 00:10:02,208
people fall in love, buy cars,
fight wars and kick a ball around.

161
00:10:05,008 --> 00:10:06,838
But even after all of this...

162
00:10:08,405 --> 00:10:10,055
...we still have the distance problem.

163
00:10:12,375 --> 00:10:14,845
AI might help us decode the message.

164
00:10:15,575 --> 00:10:17,785
We might eventually
understand their language.

165
00:10:18,495 --> 00:10:21,425
We might even learn astonishing
things about their science.

166
00:10:22,374 --> 00:10:24,784
But if they are a
thousand light-years away,

167
00:10:25,034 --> 00:10:27,304
we still can't have a normal conversation.

168
00:10:28,414 --> 00:10:31,464
The universe doesn't care how
good our translators become.

169
00:10:32,243 --> 00:10:35,433
Light still takes a thousand
years to cross that distance.

170
00:10:38,803 --> 00:10:42,013
So first contact might not look
anything like science fiction.

171
00:10:42,863 --> 00:10:48,373
There might be no face-to-face meeting.
No alien landing. No handshake.

172
00:10:49,486 --> 00:10:53,856
Perhaps first contact is simply a
faint signal arriving at a telescope...

173
00:10:55,387 --> 00:10:59,357
...followed by decades or centuries
of people trying to understand it.

174
00:10:59,963 --> 00:11:04,613
And maybe, generations later, someone
finally figures out what it says.

175
00:11:05,393 --> 00:11:07,493
Not because the aliens came to us.

176
00:11:07,573 --> 00:11:10,513
But because, for the first
time in human history, we

177
00:11:10,593 --> 00:11:13,143
discovered evidence that
somewhere in the universe...

178
00:11:15,473 --> 00:11:17,163
...someone else was there.

179
00:11:20,350 --> 00:11:22,280
And that alone would change everything.
